Let $S_{even} = \{v \mid d(v) \text{ is even}\}$ and $S_{odd} = \{v \mid d(v) \text{ is odd}\}$.
We require $|S_{even}| = |S_{odd}| = N/2$.
Note that $d(1) = 0$, which is even. So $1 \in S_{even}$.
The graph must be connected.

Let's analyze the condition based on bipartite graphs.
If a graph is bipartite, the vertices can be partitioned into two sets $A$ and $B$ such that all edges connect a vertex in $A$ to a vertex in $B$.
If the graph is connected and bipartite, then the distance from any vertex $u \in A$ to any vertex $v \in A$ is even, and the distance from $u \in A$ to $v \in B$ is odd.
Specifically, if we fix vertex 1 to be in $A$, then $S_{even} = A$ and $S_{odd} = B$.
In this case, the condition $|S_{even}| = |S_{odd}|$ is equivalent to $|A| = |B| = N/2$.
So, if the graph is bipartite, the condition holds if and only if the bipartition sizes are equal.

However, the graph might not be bipartite. If the graph contains an odd cycle, then the distance parity is not well-defined in the sense of a bipartition.
